Ladybird’s accessibility elements answer YES when AppKit calls their
accessibilityShouldUseUniqueId, so an assistive technology gets a unique
id for each element rather than its path of child indices. An element
gives its id up with NSAccessibilityUnregisterUniqueIdForUIElement when
its node leaves the tree or it’s deallocated. WebKit does the same: Its
WebAccessibilityObjectWrapper and WKAccessibilityWebPageObject answer
YES too (a comment there says “it’s faster within AppKit to look them
up”), and they unregister in WebAccessibilityObjectWrapper’s detach and
WKAccessibilityWebPageObject’s dealloc. Chrome’s AXPlatformNodeCocoa
subclasses NSAccessibilityElement, which already answers YES. Gecko’s
MOXAccessibleBase is a plain NSObject that keeps AppKit’s default, so
AppKit identifies Gecko’s elements by their path.
